# Project skill settings

`project_skill_settings` reached 110 columns because every new AI workflow added its own skill path, model, and effort columns. Postgres rejects a function call with more than 100 arguments, and Drizzle passes every column of a related row to one function, so loading that row through a relation failed in production with error 54023.

## Adding a workflow

- Never add a `*_skill_path`, `*_model`, `*_effort`, `*_bedrock_*`, or `*_score_threshold` column to `project_skill_settings`.
- Add the workflow as one entry in `PROJECT_SKILL_WORKFLOWS` (`src/shared/constants/projectSkillWorkflows.ts`). Its values live in `project_skill_workflow_settings`, one row per `(settings_id, workflow)`.
- If the registry still requires a `legacy` column mapping, step 4 of `design-docs/project-skill-settings-normalization.plan.md` has not shipped. Ship that step first. Do not add columns to work around it.
- A setting that applies to the whole project, not to one workflow, goes on the parent row only if the table stays under its ceiling. Otherwise it goes in a separate table.

`src/server/__tests__/schemaTableWidth.test.ts` fails if the table or its per-workflow columns grow. Do not raise those ceilings. Lower them when the contract migration drops columns.

## Reading settings

- Read through `projectSettingsService.ts`. It returns the flat `ProjectSkillConfig` built from the parent row and the workflow rows.
- When a relational query includes `skillSettings`, select only the columns the code uses:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
with: { skillSettings: { columns: { standupSkillPath: true, skillRepo: true, skillBranch: true } } }

// ❌ BAD: passes every column to json_build_array
with: { skillSettings: true }
```
